The page has two independent parts.

PART A (dated June 1856): Riemann's integration method for a linear hyperbolic equation in characteristic variables.

(1) The equation is L[w] = w_pq - w_q + w_p = 0. It has constant coefficients, which is what the isothermal case p = a^2 rho gives once Riemann invariants are used as independent variables.

(2) Its formal adjoint is L*[v] = v_pq + v_q - v_p = 0. Riemann imposes on v:
- on the characteristic q = q': v_p + v = 0;
- on the characteristic p = p': v_q - v = 0;
- at the vertex: v(p',q') = 1.

These are exactly the defining conditions of what is now called the Riemann function. For L[w] = w_pq + A w_p + B w_q (here A = 1, B = -1) the conditions are v_p - B v = 0 on q = q' and v_q - A v = 0 on p = p'. Riemann does not write the solution on this page. My completion: substituting v = e^{(q-q')-(p-p')} u gives u_pq + u = 0, so v = exp((q-q')-(p-p')) * J0(2*sqrt((p-p')(q-q'))).

(3) Because vL[w] - wL*[v] is a divergence, the 1-form (v_p + v) w dp + (w_q + w) v dq is exact. Equivalently, integrating by parts, the form (w - w_p) v dp + (v - v_q) w dq is exact up to d(wv).

(4) Integrating this form along a path from an initial line (the label 's' marks the data curve, with endpoints (s,p') and (s,q')) to the characteristic triangle gives the representation formula. Schematically:
- w(p',q') = w v |_(base point) + integral over the data curve of [(v_p + v) w dp + (w_q + w) v dq];
- or the equivalent symmetric version.

(5) He then differentiates with respect to q' to obtain dw'/dq'. This gives an integral over the data curve plus boundary terms (w_q + w) v at (s,q') and w dv/dq'. He rewrites it using v_p + v = 0 and similar relations. The limit notation is cramped, and the last two lines cannot be verified in detail.

This is the method published in 'Über die Fortpflanzung ebener Luftwellen von endlicher Schwingungsweite' (1860), here dated 1856.

PART B (dated October 1857): Discontinuities ('sprungweise Änderung des Drucks und der Geschwindigkeit') in an isothermal gas, p = a^2 rho. Notation: shock speed U = dx/dt; state 1 (u1, rho1) at x-0; state 2 (u2, rho2) at x+0.

(1) Mass conservation: rho1(u1-U) = rho2(u2-U) =: m.

(2) Momentum (impulse) balance: a^2(rho1-rho2) = m(u2-u1).

(3) Since u2-u1 = m(rho1-rho2)/(rho1 rho2), it follows that m^2 = a^2 rho1 rho2. Hence:
- a^2(rho1-rho2)^2 = rho1 rho2 (u1-u2)^2;
- m = a sqrt(rho1 rho2) = a^2(rho1-rho2)/(u2-u1);
- (u1-U)/a = sqrt(rho2/rho1) and (u2-U)/a = sqrt(rho1/rho2), up to a common sign;
- U = u1 ± a sqrt(rho2/rho1) = u2 ± a sqrt(rho1/rho2).

(4) Characteristic speeds: along p = const, dx/dt = u - a; along q = const, dx/dt = u + a. These are then written as (-s-1+4p)a and (s+1+4q)a, which are tied to some specific parametrisation not visible on this page.

(5) Persistence (stability) of the discontinuity. It persists if u1 > u2, that is, if it is compressive:
- if U = u1 + a sqrt(rho2/rho1) and rho1 > rho2, then u1 > u2;
- if U = u1 - a sqrt(rho2/rho1) and rho1 < rho2, then u1 > u2.
Physical conclusion: compressions steepen and eventually become discontinuous, while rarefactions spread out. For an initial disturbance of bounded extent, all condensations become shocks after a finite time.

(6) Energy balance per dt:
- change of vis viva: m(u2^2-u1^2)/2 = a^2(rho1-rho2)(u1+u2)/2;
- work done by pressure: a^2(rho1 u1 - rho2 u2);
- 'loss of vis viva' (the difference): a^2 (rho1+rho2)/2 (u1-u2), which is positive exactly for the admissible (compressive) jumps.

(7) Riemann problem for an initial jump (in modern terms):
- it splits into two shocks if u1-u2 > 0 and (u1-u2)^2 > a^2(rho1-rho2)^2/(rho1 rho2);
- into two rarefactions if u1-u2 < 0 and (u1-u2)^2 > a^2 (log(rho1/rho2))^2;
- otherwise into a forward shock plus a backward rarefaction if rho1 > rho2, with the directions reversed if rho1 < rho2.
My check: these thresholds follow from the isothermal Hugoniot curve u1 - u* = a(rho*-rho1)/sqrt(rho1 rho*) and the rarefaction curve u ± a log rho = const, evaluated at rho* = max or min(rho1, rho2).
